<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shigatse Dzong</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samdrubtse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a> Dzong, or Samdrubtse, is a fortress on a hill above Shigatse in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. It was seized by the Rinpungpa in 1435 and became the seat of their power and then of the Tsangpa kings, until the Khoshut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> captured it in 1642. Destroyed in the 1960s, it was rebuilt in the 2000s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong.jpg" alt="Shigatse Dzong" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 钉钉 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shigatse_Dzong_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The dzong, also called Samdruptse, stands on a hilltop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a> at an altitude of about 3,860 metres, near the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-tsangpo">Yarlung Tsangpo</a> and the Nyang Chu. It dominates the views of the town and of the Tashilhunpo Monastery, which Gendun Drup, the First Dalai Lama, founded near its foot in 1447. In its 17th-century form it was a smaller prototype of the Potala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, with turret-like fortifications at the ends and a central Red Palace. The modern city of Shigatse grew up around the base of the fortress.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a> was the seat of the kings of Ü-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> and the capital of the province. In 1642 the Mongol ruler Gusri Khan, supporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, defeated Prince Tsang at Shigatse and installed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> as supreme ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, after which Tashilhunpo came under the Yellow Hat sect. The fort later became the residence of the governor of Tsang.</p><p>Gurkha forces captured and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a> in 1791, before a combined Tibetan and Chinese army drove them back to the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and made them agree to keep the peace, pay tribute every five years and return what they had looted from Tashilhunpo. Until the 1950s the town was run by a dzongpön appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, while the Panchen Lamas held temporal power over three small districts nearby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The dzong was demolished in 1961, after the Tibetan uprising of 1959, and only a few ramparts were left. Between 2005 and 2007 it was rebuilt on the same site on a smaller scale with donations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, working from old photographs; the new building is of concrete, since faced with natural stone. It houses a museum of Tibetan culture and lies on the kora, the clockwise pilgrimage route that begins and ends at Tashilhunpo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shigatse Dzong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1435, after a succession crisis at the Phagmodru court, Norbu Zangpo of Rinpung seized the fortress of <b>Samdrubtse</b> (Shigatse), and the family became the real power in Tsang while still nominally serving the Phagmodru kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/rinpungpa">Rinpungpa</a></li><li><blockquote>Zhingshak Tseten Dorje, an official of the Rinpungpa at <b>Samdrubtse</b> (Shigatse), rebelled and by 1565 had replaced his masters as ruler of Tsang.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
